lesson physics-forces-on-a-sledge v=1 format=teach grade=6 unit=3 subject=physics levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Forces on a sledge"
  goal "Work out the net force on a sledge and say whether it speeds up, slows down or keeps its speed."

  look {
    scene 36x21 {
      sledgeforce s forward=[60, 40] back=[70, 30] weight=0 moving=1 show=1 names=1 at=canvas(0, 0)
      icecliff c height=2 floes=2 at=canvas(20, 11)
    }
    say """
      Every force on the sledge along the snow is an arrow in newtons. The difference between the
      forward forces and the backward ones is the net force. If it is zero the forces are balanced,
      and nothing changes: a still sledge stays still and a moving one keeps a steady speed, as this
      one does with 100 N each way. If the forces are unbalanced, the sledge speeds up or slows down,
      towards the bigger side.
      """
  }
  do {
    practice physics.sledge-net count=2 seed=1 easy-seed=2 hard-seed=3
    level easy {
      worked physics.sledge-motion v=0 level=medium
    }
    practice physics.sledge-motion count=1 seed=1 easy-seed=2 hard-seed=3
    practice physics.sledge-steady-pull count=1 seed=1 easy-seed=2 hard-seed=3
    practice physics.sledge-start count=1 seed=1 easy-seed=2 hard-seed=3
    practice physics.sledge-weight count=1 seed=1 easy-seed=2 hard-seed=3
    practice physics.sledge-surface count=1 seed=1 easy-seed=2 hard-seed=3
    practice physics.sledge-dogs count=1 seed=1 easy-seed=2 hard-seed=3
  }
  story {
    show physics.sledge-sea-ice v=1 easy-v=0 hard-v=2
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show physics.sledge-steady-reason v=0 hard-v=1
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show physics.sledge-rest-dogs v=1 easy-v=0 hard-v=3
  }
  remember {
    say "Balanced forces keep a sledge's speed the same; only a net force speeds it up or slows it down."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea to listen for is that a moving sledge needs a bigger forward force to keep going. It
    does not: with the forces balanced it keeps a steady speed, and a bigger pull would make it speed
    up. The two-star question asks exactly this. The second idea is that friction is always the same
    size; on a still sledge it only pushes back as hard as it is pulled, up to its most. If the
    arrows confuse the child, add the forward ones, add the backward ones, and take one from the
    other before saying anything about the motion. Off the page, push a book across a table at a
    steady speed and feel how hard you have to keep pushing, then try it on a tray of dried peas.
    The three-star question is solved in two parts, the start and then the steady run.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level there is one force holding the sledge back, a worked example says what a sledge does, the loads are light, the dog team never lands exactly on the friction, and the sea ice question has the smallest numbers."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level friction and the air both hold the sledge back, the motion question includes a sledge standing still, the dog teams land exactly on the friction so one more is needed, and in the three-star question seven dogs start the sledge."
  }
}
